<div class="section" id="Ensuring-Long_002dTerm-Source-Code-Archiving">
<div class="header">
<p>
Next: <a href="Referencing-the-Software-Environment.html" accesskey="n" rel="next">Step 4: Referencing the Software Environment</a>, Previous: <a href="Recording-the-Environment.html" accesskey="p" rel="prev">Step 2: Recording the Environment</a>, Up: <a href="Reproducible-Research.html" accesskey="u" rel="up">Using Guix for Reproducible Research</a> &nbsp; [<a href="index.html#SEC_Contents" title="Table of contents" rel="contents">Contents</a>][<a href="Terminy-i-ukazateli.html" title="Index" rel="index">Index</a>]</p>
</div>
<hr />
<span id="Step-3_003a-Ensuring-Long_002dTerm-Source-Code-Archiving"></span><h3 class="section">9.3 Step 3: Ensuring Long-Term Source Code Archiving</h3>

<p>We insisted on version control before: for the <samp>manifest.scm</samp> and
<samp>channels.scm</samp> files, but of course also for your own code. Our
recommendation is to have these two <samp>.scm</samp> files in the same repository
as the code they&rsquo;re about.
</p>
<p>Since the goal is enabling reproducibility, source code availability is a
prime concern.  Source code hosting services come and go and we don&rsquo;t want
our code to vanish in a whim and render our published research work
unverifiable. <a href="https://www.softwareheritage.org/">Software Heritage</a>
(SWH for short) is <em>the</em> solution for this: SWH archives public source
code and provides unique intrinsic identifiers to refer to
it&mdash;<a href="https://swhid.org"><em>SWHIDs</em></a>.  Guix itself is
<a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/3641525.3663622">connected to SWH</a> to (1)&nbsp;ensure that the source code of its packages is archived, and (2)&nbsp;to fall
back to downloading from the SWH archive should code vanish from its
original site.
</p>
<p>Once your own code is available in a public version-control repository, such
as a Git repository on your lab&rsquo;s hosting service, you can ask SWH to
archive it by going to its
<a href="https://archive.softwareheritage.org/save/">Save Code Now</a> interface.
SWH will process the request asynchronously and eventually you&rsquo;ll find your
code has made it into <a href="https://archive.softwareheritage.org/">the
archive</a>.
</p>
</div>



